<Base title="About" section="about">
  <header class="article-head">
    <h1>About</h1>
  </header>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      I’m Rebecca Fordon. Before I was a librarian I practiced as a corporate bankruptcy attorney, which is where I
      first got interested in how technology changes legal work.
    </p>
    <p>
      I teach legal research, legal writing, and legal technology, and I speak often on generative AI in the law. Much
      of my writing since 2023 has been about one question: if AI can make things up, how should
      lawyers, students, and librarians work with it? That led to essays on hallucinations and retrieval-augmented
      generation, to classroom experiments, and eventually to building my own small tools, most of them over evenings and weekends.
    </p>
    <p>
      Since 2023, I've been writing at <em><a href="https://www.ailawlibrarians.com/">AI Law Librarians</a></em>, a group blog I co-founded with fellow law librarians.
      My posts from that site are <a href="/blog/">republished here</a> with their original dates.
    </p>
    <h2>A note on affiliation</h2>
    <p>
      This is a personal site. Some of the tools I describe were built for courses. Opinions here, including reviews of research products, are mine and not my employer's.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
